A wealth of nucleic acids, including antisense single-stranded DNA oligonucleotide and small interfering RNA, are used for gene interference therapy in clinical practice.[1-3] Since naked nucleic acids are unable to cross cell membranes and are susceptible to rapid degradation, current gene delivery systems are often compromised by low therapeutic performance.[1-3] Recently, codelivery of nucleic acid therapeutics and small molecular drugs with nanoparticles (NPs) is emerging as a promising strategy to maximize the benefit of combination therapies by simultaneously regulating multiple troublesome pathways.[4,5] However, these two types of agents possess significantly different physicochemical properties and, therefore, are rather difficult to co-load in a single nanoparticle with specific ratios and high efficiency.
As a type of coordination polymers, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are built by coordination-driven selfassembly of metal ions and organic molecules[6,7] and have attracted great attention for encapsulation and delivery of small molecular drugs.[8-11] The MOF backbone itself can be functionalized in multiple ways to develop more efficient nanocarriers.[12,13] Most recently, MOFs have also demonstrated great potential as nanocarriers for functional biomacromolecules, e.g. nucleic acids,[14,15] proteins,[16-22] and nucleic acid-protein combinations.[23,24] Current immobilization strategies are often carried out through the post-synthetic infiltration of biomacromolecules into the MOFs with large pores[16-19] or adsorption of biomacromolecules on their surface.[14] These approaches are often limited by the restriction of the pore size and possible degradation of cargos by exterior surroundings. Recently, encapsulation of biomacromolecules in MOF NPs with small pore size was achieved via a mild de novo approach, such as biomimetic mineralization.[15,20-25] Despite the progress made, these delivery systems suffer from low drug loading efficiency (DLE) and low drug loading content (DLC, usually less than $10\%$ ) in MOF NPs. The former enables waste of expensive drugs, while low DLC causes poor therapeutic outcome and high potential toxicity of exogenous materials (for making nanocarriers). Moreover, few effort has been made to exploit codelivery systems. Development of simple approaches for controlled co-loading of biomacromolecule therapeutics (e.g. nucleic acids)  and small molecule drugs in MOF-based biomimetic system with highly augmented DLC and DLE remains a great challenge.
In this study, to overcome the aforementioned issues, we describe an extremely simple and facile strategy to construct a coordination polymer-based nanoplatform to achieve ultraefficient encapsulation and codelivery of nucleic acid therapeutics (G3139) and chemotherapeutic drug doxorubicin (DOX) (Scheme 1). With the aid of Fe(II) ions, these two types of therapeutic agents co-assembled into uniform coordination NPs (DOX/Fe-G). The obtained NPs are then mineralized with a thin shell of ZIF-8 MOFs to yield the final core-shell structured nanoplatform, referred to as DOX/Fe-G@Z. The use of extra synthetic carriers can be circumvented in our strategy, thereby resulting in an ultra-high loading of small molecular drugs and nucleic acid therapeutics in the NPs. Ultimately, the nanoplatform renders augmented anticancer capacity and allows for in vivo MR imaging. The straightforward biomimetic approach engenders broad opportunities for practical use.[12,26]
