G3139, an antisense oligonucleotide targets the antiapoptotic protein Bcl-2,[27] was chosen as an nucleic acid therapeutic for codelivery with DOX. DOX/Fe-G were synthesized simply by mixing water solutions of G3139, DOX, and Fe(II) ions $(\mathsf{G3139}/\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{I I})/\mathsf{D O X}$ at molar ratio of 1:40:11), followed by incubation at $95^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 h. After centrifugation, the resulting NPs were facilely obtained. Figure 1a presents the representative transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of the obtained DOX/Fe-G. Uniform and spherical nanoparticles with smooth surface were clearly observed. In nature, biomineral exteriors are adopted by many organisms to improve their physicochemical stability. We then mineralized the obtained DOX/Fe-G with a shell of ZIF-8, a biocompatible MOF synthesized by self-assembly of Zn(II) ions and 2- methylimidazole. As shown in Figure 1b-d, the surface mineralization is identified with the growth of the rough ZIF-8 shell on the individual NPs. The investigation of DOX/Fe-G@Z with HAADF-STEM-EDS clearly illustrated that phosphorus (from nucleic acids) and Fe were homogeneously distributed in the core, while Zn (from ZIF-8) were located at the shell (Figure 1e). The elements distribution within the core-shell NP is further verified by the EDS line scanning (Figure 1f). The average diameter of the NPs changed from $144\pm18$ nm to $173\pm20$ nm after the surface mineralization (Figure S1). The measurement of zeta potential indicated that DOX/Fe-G were negatively charged $(-2.19\mathrm{\mV})$ and became positively charged $(+10.05$ mV) after the surface coating (Figure 1g).
Remarkably, the approach allows to encapsulate G3139 and DOX in an ultra-efficient and precisely controllable manner. DOX and G3139 of different ratios (3.5:1, 7:1, 11:1 and 14.5:1) were selected for the synthesis. Notably, the NPs synthesized under such conditions retain the spherical shape and size uniformity (Figure S2). Upon increasing the DOX:G3139 ratio from 3.5:1 to 14.5:1, DLC of DOX increased from $25.7~\mathsf{w t\%}$ to $63.7~\mathsf{w t\%}$ , while
DLC of G3139 decreased from $57.3~\mathsf{w t\%}$ to $25.6~\mathsf{w t\%}$ (Figure 1h). The DOX:G3139 ratio in the nanoparticles (ranging from 4.12 to 23.3) is tunable based on the tailoring of their initial ratio added during the preparation (Figure 1i). Moreover, both the DLE of DOX and G3139 were higher than $80\%$ when the initial molar ratio of DOX/G3139 was lower than 11.0 (Figure 1j). Such DLC and DLE are significantly higher than those obtained by traditional nanocarrier-based delivery systems. This is because that DOX and G3139 used here not only act as cargos but also participate in the assembly of the NPs through cooperative interactions.
The stability of NPs is an important but formidable challenge for coordination polymer-based drug delivery systems because of the fragility of noncovalent interactions. The stabilities of Fe-G (the Fe-G3139 NPs without loading with DOX, Figure S3) and DOX/Fe-G (DOX:G3139 ratio of 23.3) were investigated. The results show that both Fe-G and DOX/Fe-G are stable in water or HEPES buffer (Figure S4). In contrast, decomposition of Fe-G and DOX/Fe-G occurred at 10 min and 6 h in PBS (Figure S5 and Figure S6), respectively. The instability of the NPs in PBS can be attributed to the competitive coordination of phosphate ions with iron ions, weakening the coordination interactions within the NPs. Notably, the introduction of DOX in the NPs can significantly improve the stability of the resulted DOX/Fe-G in PBS (Figure S5), confirming that the incorporation of cooperative interactions facilitates to engineer stable nanodrugs. As expected, the surface mineralization could further improve the stability of the NPs in PBS, and no obvious decomposition happened until $12\mathsf{h}$ for DOX/Fe-G@Z (Figure S6).
We next investigated the in vitro release of DOX from the DOX/Fe-G and DOX/Fe-G@Z. The drug release is negligible in water and HEPES buffer after 12 h incubation (Figure S7), consisted with the stability data mentioned above. Interestingly, the drug release is more sustained from the DOX/Fe-G@Z than DOX/Fe-G in PBS (Figure S8), suggesting that the ZIF-8 shell reduces the spontaneous drug release. Since high DLC in the NPs leads to the highly fluorescence quenching of DOX (Figure S9), the gradual release of DOX from DOX/Fe-G@Z resulted in a recovery of its fluorescence intensity (Figure S10).
Next, DOX/Fe-G@Z were investigated for cellular codelivery and combined therapy. Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) images show that the fluorescence intensity of DOX and G3139 (G3139 was labeled with Cy5) in MCF-7 cells gradually increased with the treatment time (Figure 2a), suggesting a endocytosis-mediated uptake of the DOX/Fe-G@Z. Quantitative flow cytometric analysis confirmed that the fluorescence intensity of G3139 and DOX after 6 h incubation was 18.9-fold and 4.7-fold higher than that of cells at an early uptake stage of $0.5{\ h}$ , respectively (Figure 2b,c). In contrast, CLSM revealed no obvious florescence in the cells incubated with free G3139 (Figure S11), which could be attributed to the poor cell membrane permeability of naked nucleic acids. Flow cytometry analysis revealed that the cells treated with DOX/Fe-G@Z exbibited 12.4- and 19.5-fold higher Cy5 fluorescence intensity than those incubated with free G3139 for 1 h and 6 h, respectively (Figure S12), suggesting that the nanoplatform could significantly improve the cellular uptake of therapeutic nucleic acids. Furthermore, the nanoplatform shows an enhanced gene silencing activity (Figure S13) and improved activation of the cell-apoptosis marker of caspase-3 (Figure S14, Figure S15). As shown in Figure 2d, treatment with DOX/Fe$\mathsf{G@Z}$ induced higher cytotoxicity than that of the cells incubated with Fe-G@Z or DOX/Fe-nG@Z (synthesized using a nonG3139 sequence), confirming the combination effect of chemotherapy and antisense gene therapy.
Given the magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) capability of iron ions, we explored the potential MRI application of DOX/Fe${\mathsf{G}}{\ @}{Z}$ . The transverse relaxation time of DOX/Fe-G@Z with different Fe concentrations was recorded in Figure 3a. Through a linear regression curve fitting of inverse relaxation times $(1/\mathsf{T}_{2})$ plotted against the Fe concentration, the transversal relaxivity $\left(\mathsf{R}_{2}\right)$ of $\mathsf{D O X/F e–G@Z}$ was calculated to be $54.8\ m\mathsf{M}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . This value is comparable with that of Fe-based MOF materials.[28] Interestingly, the ${\sf R}_{2}$ value of the DOX/Fe-G@Z was 4.5-fold larger than that of Fe-G@Z, indicating that the incorporation of DOX can significantly improve the MRI capability. A concentration-dependent $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted MR images could be clearly observed (insets in Figure 5a). Therefore, the NPs showed their potential as theranostic platforms for MR imagingguided combination therapy. To examine the MRI performance of DOX/Fe-G@Z in vivo, $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted MR images were acquired on nude mice bearing MCF-7 tumors at different times after the intravenous injection. As depicted in Figure 3b and Figure S16, a significant contrast increase of $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted MR signal intensity was observed in the tumor within 6 h and then decreased, suggesting effective accumulation of DOX/Fe-G@Z in tumor through passive targeting.
We further investigated whether the nanoplatform would promote G3139 delivery in vivo. The MCF-7 breast tumorbearing mice were treated with Cy5-labled $F e-G@Z$ or free G3139 by tail-vein injection. As shown in Figure 3c, a maximal intratumoral accumulation of the NPs was observed at $6\mathfrak{h}$ postadministration, which is consistent with previous MRI results. The fluorescence in the tumor was detectable even at $24\ h$ after injection, indicating efficient delivery of the G3139 to tumors. By contrast, minimum intratumoral fluorescence could be detected at 6 h post-injection in the mice treated with free G3139. Quantitative analysis showed that the intratumoral accumulation of $F e-G@Z$ was about 7.5 times higher than that of free G3139 at 6 h after injection (Figure 3d). To investigate the biodistribution of the NPs, the organs and tumors were harvested at $6\mathfrak{h}$ post-injection for ex vivo imaging (Figure 3e,f). The results further confirmed that the NPs were accumulated largely in mouse tumors via passive targeted delivery.
Encouraged by the efficient delivery of the nanosystem in vivo, we subsequently investigated their antitumor efficacy. The MCF-7 breast tumor-bearing mice were intravenously administrated with different samples. As shown in Figure 4a,b, Figure S17, and Figure S18, the tumor growth of mice injected with Fe-G@Z or DOX/Fe-nG@Z was efficiently delayed due to gene therapy and chemotherapy, respectively. In the mice treated with DOX/Fe-G@Z, the tumor growth was completely suppressed, confirming that the combination therapy was more effective than either modality alone. As a control, there was no inhibition effect on tumor for the group treated with $F e\cdot n G@Z$ . The hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining and the in situ TUNEL assay of tumor sections showed that the combination therapy induced much higher level of nucleus necrosis and apoptosis than control groups (Figure 4c). Moreover, the images obtained using the caspase-3 immunofluorescence staining showed the highest caspase-3 activation effect in the tumor harvested from DOX/Fe-G@Z group (Figure 4c). No significant change in body weight of mice was observed in any group during the treatment (Figure S19). The biocompatibility of the DOX/Fe-G@Z was also confirmed by H&E staining analysis of internal organs (Figure S20).
In conclusion, we have developed in this work a biomimetic biomineralization strategy that is versatile and extremely simple to encapsulate drug-nucleic acid combinations within a single NP platform for the cancer therapy. The NPs possess uniform sizes, high stability, and high DLE and DLC with precisely controlled ratio of two therapeutic agents. The nanoplatform achieves efficient co-delivery and exhibits effective tumor growth inhibition in vitro and in vivo. The simplicity of the biomimetic approach makes it amenable to codelivery of other nucleic acid therapeutics and small molecular drugs.
